## Some examples

Consider the system of equations given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
    x - 2y &= -4 \\
    -2x + 4y &= 8
\end{aligned}
$$

Any vector $v = (x,y)$ such that $x = 2y - 4$ will solve the above system

Since we can find infinite such vectors this system has infinitely many solutions

This is because the rows of the corresponding matrix are linearly dependent


## Nonsingular matrices

+ A square matrix $A$ is nonsingular if and only if the rows and columns of $A$ are linearly independent

+ To every square matrix we can assign a unique number called the [determinant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinant).

+ For $2 \times 2$ matrices, the determinant is given by,

::: {.fragment}

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
    \color{red}{a} & \color{blue}{b} \\
    \color{blue}{c} & \color{red}{d}
\end{bmatrix}
=
{\color{red}{ad}} - {\color{blue}{bc}}
$$

:::

+ the determinant of a $3 \times 3$ matrix

::: {.fragment}

$$
det \begin{bmatrix}
    a & b & c \\
    d & e & f \\
    g & h & i
\end{bmatrix}
= a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg)
$$

:::

+ If the determinant of $A$ is not zero, then we say that $A$ is *nonsingular*

::: {.notes}

* The determinant of a $2 \times 2$ matrix is also the area of the parallelogram spanned by the column vectors

* The determinant of a $2 \times 2$ matrix is also the factor by which the matrix scales the area of any set of points in the plane
    + if the determinant is negative, the matrix also flips the orientation of the plane
    + if the determinant is zero, the matrix collapses the plane to a line or lower-dimensional space
    + if the determinant is one, the matrix preserves the area of the plane
    + if the determinant is greater than one, the matrix expands the area of the plane
    + if the determinant is less than one, the matrix shrinks the area of the plane



